  "rationale": "Reuses the pinned c-toxcore case: toxav places a call to a chosen friend_number, answers it, reports a call-state bitmask (sending/accepting audio and video, FINISHED) and exposes mute, hide-video and cancel controls, while Messenger.h holds the friend list, names and online status. Those map onto the leaf's selected-counterpart live call plus persistent social context. Target is a small browser app with WebRTC media and a tiny signaling/persistence service; the GPL C library is a structural reference only and no code is copied.",
  "reuse_case_id": "case:repo:b7df8a52d1ed856bfc163998:2f9a763de29d8fab4e6bd1fafd8476fb22e1322c",
  "delivery_profile": "web",
  "scenario": {
   "name": "Evening call with a chosen friend, logged so it can be redialed",
   "audience": "An individual using the app in personal leisure time",
   "objective": "Place a live two-way video call to a friend chosen from the contact list, toggle one's own camera and microphone, end the call, then redial from the resulting history entry.",
   "workflow": [
    "Sign in as Mara; the conversation list shows her profile, friend Deniz with online presence, and one earlier call entry.",
    "Select Deniz and place a video call; the stage shows a ringing state until Deniz answers.",
    "Deniz answers; both stages show the other person's live video and their own self-view, each with personal camera and microphone toggles.",
    "Mara turns her camera off, sees her own tile paused, and Deniz sees her video paused; she turns it back on.",
    "Deniz ends the call; the stage closes and a history entry with counterpart, start time, duration and outcome appears for both.",
    "Mara reopens that history entry and redials Deniz from it."
   ],
   "acceptance": [
    "After the call is answered, each participant sees live remote video plus their own self-view, and no recording or replay is produced.",
    "A camera or microphone toggle changes only that participant's outbound media and is visibly reflected on the other participant's stage.",
    "Ending the call writes exactly one history entry per participant containing counterpart, start time, duration and outcome, and that entry can start a new call."
   ]
  }
 },
